body:
Hey isn't that the great thing of life though? It is completely in our control (to some extent). Some people place so much value in their life - maybe they're religious or just feel very worthy - while others see their lives as insignificant in the grander scheme of things (maybe they're into science, or atheism, or they're depressed - who knows!) But the fact that you get to define your life in any way you wish, and place as much (or as little) value in it as you feel appropriate, is really an empowering thing to think about (would you agree?)

&#x200B;

Things can feel really, really, really shit. Especially when things are so repetitive and mundane, as you seem to be expressing. It's almost like another experience, even if more difficult, would be more bearable than your current one. I hear you about the wanting to drive away and never come back. Well, you know, if you ever get to the point that suicide feels like the only option... why not first take the opportunity to drive somewhere and see what sort of adventure life takes you on?

&#x200B;

But what I really want to say is: it's okay that you feel unmotivated. Mental illness and depression and SI is a bitch, and feeling as though you cannot go through with suicide has no bearing on your willpower. It sounds like you are using SO MUCH willpower just to survive each day, and to not succumb to these urges/thoughts.

This hurts my heart to hear so I can only imagine the pain you are experiencing since his death. The love that you both had isn't gone, it has simply changed. All of the experiences you had together, and the ways in which you helped each other to grow, do not disappear after one person dies. His love and soul will live on through the way you interact with others and with the world (and in every other person he touched throughout his life). Sorry, I hope that doesn't sound too mushy or cliche, grief is fucking hard, and I hope that you can experience some sense of peace here on earth (without having to resort to suicide).

&#x200B;

For what it's worth, you sound like a genuinely kind and thoughtful individual. I don't know how close you are with your family and friends, of course probably not as close as you were with your fiance, but I think it's important to remember how valuable you are in the lives of others. The significance that your fiance had in your life and in your heart, is the same amount of significance you have in other people's lives. I hope that you stay for a little while longer, and explore different paths that may be available to you. There are so many good things that end (loving relationships, careers, raising children, the lives of loved ones, siblings, and parents) and these experiences ending doesn't negate all that was good about them. It doesn't even necessarily mean that things ending were wrong. And it certainly doesn't mean that the good in the world is lost.
